  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the key uncertainty is whether the full multimodal + multiprofile pipeline is the right direction and how much each component contributes. config_013 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) tests the core hypothesis that combining RNA and image modalities with multi-profile aggregation improves accuracy. It uses a compact learned RNA encoder (scvi_n200, 200-dim) paired with a strong pretrained image encoder (ViT-L), simple average-pool aggregation (cheap baseline before transformer), and standard concat fusion. This establishes a strong reference point: if it performs well, subsequent experiments can ablate components (e.g., drop to singleprofile, swap to resnet50, try transformer aggregation) to understand what drives performance; if it underperforms, I can pivot to simpler or single-modality configurations. Expected relative cost: moderate-to-high (ViT-L is the most expensive image encoder, but avgpool aggregation and the compact 200-dim scvi latent keep the overall training cost manageable compared to transformer-aggregation or scgpt-based configs)."
}

